WEBVTT

00:00:00.496 --> 00:00:02.238
A room full of donated supplies.

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.968
But how many complete school kits?

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:10.048
KitBridge turns mixed donations into a packing plan, without breaking promises.

00:00:12.008 --> 00:00:13.436
Start with the supplies you have.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:15.735
Then define what each grade group needs.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.225
Four kits are promised to each group.

00:00:20.492 --> 00:00:22.280
That uses all twelve notebooks.

00:00:25.485 --> 00:00:27.133
Notebooks are the bottleneck.

00:00:27.714 --> 00:00:30.303
Four more lower-grade kits need four more notebooks.

00:00:30.976 --> 00:00:32.950
One five-dollar pack contains five.

00:00:33.531 --> 00:00:37.884
The optimizer reaches twelve complete kits, then minimizes the purchase cost.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.013
Reduce the budget to four dollars and update the plan.

00:00:42.594 --> 00:00:45.682
No useful purchase fits, so spending stays at zero.

00:00:47.308 --> 00:00:50.385
With only seven backpacks, eight promised kits are impossible.

00:00:50.965 --> 00:00:52.324
KitBridge shows the shortage.

00:00:52.753 --> 00:00:54.692
It never silently lowers your commitment.

00:00:56.129 --> 00:01:00.019
Switch between what you can pack now and what to pack after supplies arrive.

00:01:00.692 --> 00:01:02.585
Each group gets item quantities.

00:01:03.072 --> 00:01:06.451
Export the plan, or print the lists for the packing table.

00:01:08.141 --> 00:01:08.861
KitBridge.

00:01:09.534 --> 00:01:10.811
Make every supply count.
